:[\"cmd-ad685b7ab8824c02\",\"cmd-ec50b67d5242415c\"]},{\"id\":\"cert-auto-renew\",\"requirement\":\"Certificate is renewed automatically before it expires\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A renew-cert.sh script (24h validity, renews when <20h remain) is installed and driven by an enabled/active systemd timer (cert-renew.timer, 1-minute interval, Persistent=true). The script's renewal branch was exercised directly (issuing a new cert and reloading nginx) and its no-op branch was also exercised (skips renewal when cert is still valid, confirmed via renew.log 'need_renew=0'), demonstrating correct logic. The timer's own unattended firing within the actual 20h renewal window was not observed (only a manually forced run and time-boxed monitoring were captured), which is an inherent limitation of a bounded evidence window rather than a gap in the mechanism.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-57943aae49af44fc\",\"cmd-096583217d0d4815\",\"cmd-eb7c45d612df49a3\",\"cmd-44011d3636174858\"]},{\"id\":\"live-rotation-no-downtime\",\"requirement\":\"Service picks up each new certificate while continuing to answer requests (no downtime during rotation)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A 200-request curl loop against :8443 was run in the background while a forced renewal was triggered mid-loop; the certificate serial changed (C3F5AFA1F3B51404 -> CD6D0634AC90C5F6) via nginx reload, and all 200 requests returned HTTP 200 with zero failures, directly demonstrating gapless pickup of the new certificate.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-bf1143c7cb884599\",\"cmd-e31e6d6f14744736\",\"cmd-82d021ae68444c7a\",\"cmd-42253e3cd4694b8f\",\"cmd-81a0e868528a4dd6\"]},{\"id\":\"node-restart-protocol\",\"requirement\":\"(Independent) provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart baseline captured boot_id and healthy nginx/timer/HTTPS state; shutdown -r now was issued and acknowledged, a subsequent command failed with connection refused confirming disconnection, boot_id was observed to change post-reboot, and post-restart checks show nginx active, cert-renew.timer active+enabled, HTTPS 8443 returning 200, and the same valid certificate persisted on disk. This is cross-checked by the fixed after-prepare (boot_id 8336c3a1...) vs after-executor (boot_id 87f91775...) snapshots, which independently confirm the boot identity change.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-7a0bfcd7e8cb4bcf\",\"cmd-f9ef8fb64f0c42b3\",\"cmd-c8d2a60561d64fe1\",\"cmd-f61a67db4cea47ad\",\"cmd-b4e22964816f4e6f\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.9,\"summary\":\"Executor commands were closely tied to the task: nginx install, cert script, systemd units, and site config were all necessary and remain in place with no evidence of residue or conflicting services (after-executor snapshot shows no failed-services and only expected systemd-private tmp entries tied to the new boot). The one notable state-changing detour was temporarily patching renew-cert.sh with a FORCE=1 test hook (cmd-f2cc5c3336b24960) to demonstrate live rotation without waiting ~20h; this was a bounded, low-risk mutation that was fully reverted afterward (cmd-44011d3636174858), with the restored script verified to correctly no-op on a still-valid cert. Because it was necessary to produce evidence for the no-downtime-rotation requirement and was fully restored, it is not treated as an unnecessary mutation. No destructive, broad, or security-weakening actions were observed; the earlier failed unprivileged apt-get attempt (cmd-317adf642521476c) was a harmless permission-denied discovery step with no state change.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-f2cc5c3336b24960\",\"cmd-44011d3636174858\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outco